<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Abdallab Sheikhdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1504 CE – 1821 CE (approx.) · capital Qerri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Arab sheikhdom of Qerri, partner of the Funj sultans, which governed the northern Sudanese Nile as their chief vassal for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Abdallab descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-jamma">Abdallah Jamma</a>, leader of the Qawasma <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, who allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amara-dunqas">Amara Dunqas</a> to destroy <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alodia">Alodia</a> around 1504. Abdallah established his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qerri">Qerri</a>, north of the confluence of the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Niles</a>, controlling the routes north toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Abdallab chiefs, titled manjil, ruled the Arab tribes and the northern provinces as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a>, collecting tribute and controlling trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajib-al-kafuta">Ajib al-Kafuta</a>, the most famous chief, sought greater independence and was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a> around 1611; afterwards the sheikhs remained subordinate but influential.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Abdallab lost much of their power in the 18th century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a> regents dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a>. In 1821 they submitted to the invading army of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/egypt-under-muhammad-ali">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a> absorbed their lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1504 CE – 1821 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.63%;width:6.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1504 CE – 1821 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qerri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdallah Jamma, Ajib al-Kafuta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abdallab, Kingdom of Qerri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=abdallab-sheikhdom" aria-label="Abdallab Sheikhdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5569 1574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Abdallab Sheikhdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5889 1830L5883 1851"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5883 1851h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5889 1830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5889 1830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Qerri. <a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=abdallab-sheikhdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

